Um I just trained this way because it
made the most intuitive sense to me. And

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.280 align:start position:19%
I've played around with a variety of
different approaches over the years, but

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:49.360 align:start position:19%
this is the one that I always come back
to because it is very effective and very

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:52.520 align:start position:19%
reliable. So, uh double progression,
what does that mean? Very simply, it

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.720 align:start position:19%
just means that you're adjusting two
different variables as you progress

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.920 align:start position:19%
through your training, which are weight
and reps. You have a given rep range. Uh

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:04.040 align:start position:19%
let's use eight to 10 reps as an example
here. Um and let's say you're doing

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:08.440 align:start position:19%
three sets of a given exercise. So, your
first goal is to train for additional

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:13.239 align:start position:19%
reps with whatever weight you're using.
Then, once you can hit uh the upper end

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.959 align:start position:19%
of your rep range across all three sets,
uh so, in this case, it would be 10

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.400 align:start position:19%
reps, you would increase the weight and
start the process over again. So, maybe

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.959 align:start position:19%
it looks something like this as an
example. For workout one, uh on the

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:27.560 align:start position:19%
first set, you do 100 lbs for 10 reps.
Then, let's say on the second set, you

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:31.519 align:start position:19%
get nine reps, and on the third set,
eight reps. Workout number two, you come

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.000 align:start position:19%
back and you do 100 lbs for 10 reps.
Then, you get 10 reps on the second set

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.239 align:start position:19%
this time, and then nine reps on the
third. Now, for workout number three,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:43.880 align:start position:19%
you do 10 reps across all three sets.
So, now, for workout four, you add 5 to

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:47.040 align:start position:19%
10 lbs, depending on the exercise, uh
and maybe again you do 10 reps, nine

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.400 align:start position:19%
reps, eight reps, and you just keep
progressing forward like this. So, you

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.760 align:start position:19%
train for reps, and then once you can do
10 reps across all three sets, you bump

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.520 align:start position:19%
up the weight and you go back to
training for reps again. And keep in

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:00.600 align:start position:19%
mind that these rep counts are just
examples. Uh the number of reps that you

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:03.680 align:start position:19%
lose or don't lose from set to set,
that's going to depend on exactly how

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.520 align:start position:19%
hard you're training and your strength
levels on any given day. So, that's a

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.600 align:start position:19%
basic double progression model, okay?
Pretty straightforward. And it does work

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.880 align:start position:19%
well. Now, with uh dynamic double
progression, you're going to do things a

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.600 align:start position:19%
little bit differently. And that's that
you don't have to hit the upper end of

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:22.040 align:start position:19%
your rep range across all three sets
before you go ahead and increase the

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.440 align:start position:19%
weight. Instead, what you're going to do
is maintain the same relative effort

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:29.960 align:start position:19%
level across all three sets. Um what I
recommend is about one to two reps short

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:34.200 align:start position:19%
of failure. Um if you're thinking in
terms of RPE, then that would be an RPE

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.919 align:start position:19%
of about eight to nine. And you're going
to use the first set of each exercise as

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:42.080 align:start position:19%
your main gauge for progression. So, the
first set is the one that you really

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.880 align:start position:19%
focus on and that you really use to
determine when it's time to add more

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:50.360 align:start position:19%
weight. Um as soon as you can hit the
upper end of your rep range on that

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:54.400 align:start position:19%
first set, then the following workout,
the weight on that first set. And then

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:58.720 align:start position:19%
for the remaining sets of that exercise,
you can reduce the weight if necessary

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:02.280 align:start position:19%
in order to stay within your targeted
rep range. You don't have to use the

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:05.440 align:start position:19%
same weight across every single set with
this approach. So, here's an example of

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:09.440 align:start position:19%
what this might look like. Again, we're
going to use a rep range of eight to 10

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.160 align:start position:19%
with three sets for the lift. So, for
workout number one, you do 100 lb for

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.600 align:start position:19%
eight reps. And since you're at the
bottom end of that eight to 10 rep

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:21.520 align:start position:19%
range, you decrease the weight slightly
to stay within the range. So, for set

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:26.280 align:start position:19%
number two, uh you do 95 lb for say nine
reps, and then 95 lb for eight reps.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.440 align:start position:19%
Workout number two, you do 100 lb for
nine reps. And now since you got nine

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.520 align:start position:19%
reps, you can probably get eight on the
second set. And so, you stick with 100

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:38.240 align:start position:19%
lb and you do eight reps. Now, you're at
the bottom end of the range, so you

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:42.840 align:start position:19%
decrease to 95 lb and you get nine reps.
Workout number three, you do 100 lb for

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:47.720 align:start position:19%
10 reps, then 100 lb for nine reps, then
100 lb for eight reps. And now for

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:51.960 align:start position:19%
workout four, since you got 100 lbs for
10 reps on that first set, you increase

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:56.160 align:start position:19%
to 105. Okay, you don't continue using
100 lbs on the other two sets and wait

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.320 align:start position:19%
until they all reach 10 reps like you
would with the previous double

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.760 align:start position:19%
progression model. So now you do 105 lbs
for eight reps. You're at the bottom end

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.120 align:start position:19%
of the range, so you drop down to 100
lbs for nine reps and then 100 lbs for

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:12.960 align:start position:19%
eight reps. Workout number five, you do
105 lbs for nine reps, then 105 lbs for

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.240 align:start position:19%
eight reps, and then you drop down to
100 lbs and you do nine reps.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:33.520 align:start position:19%
So hopefully you get the idea here.
Again, you're using the first set of the

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:37.640 align:start position:19%
exercise as your primary gauge for when
to add more weight. And once you can hit

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:41.160 align:start position:19%
the upper end of your rep range on that
first set, you increase the weight on

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:45.160 align:start position:19%
the first set for the following workout.
And then from there, if you need to drop

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.840 align:start position:19%
the weight down slightly on one or both
of the following sets uh to stay within

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:51.760 align:start position:19%
your targeted rep range, since um it's
normal to lose a rep or two if you're

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:56.280 align:start position:19%
really training hard enough, then that's
totally fine. So again, both the basic

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:59.960 align:start position:19%
double progression approach and the
dynamic double progression approach uh

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:03.200 align:start position:19%
they're both effective, but with the
dynamic model, I would say that you'll

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.560 align:start position:19%
likely progress faster in the overall
picture because you're keeping the

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:11.080 align:start position:19%
relative effort level the same across
all of your sets rather than allowing

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:14.919 align:start position:19%
the effort level to drop on the first or
second set or maybe even the third and

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.919 align:start position:19%
fourth set if you're doing uh say four
to five sets for an exercise. For

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:24.200 align:start position:19%
example, if you're using the first model
and you do 10 reps, nine reps, eight

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:28.680 align:start position:19%
reps for workout one, and then you do uh
10 reps, 10 reps, nine reps on workout

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:32.000 align:start position:19%
two, if you've already done uh 10 reps
on the first set two weeks in a row,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.680 align:start position:19%
then I would say that you're leaving
progress on the table by once again

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:39.760 align:start position:19%
going back for week three and doing the
exact same thing on that first set when

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.520 align:start position:19%
you are uh fully fresh and when your
strength is at its highest. And you're

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:47.480 align:start position:19%
only leaving that progression to that
third and final set. The relative effort

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:51.320 align:start position:19%
on that first set is dropping from week
to week as you wait for the subsequent

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.760 align:start position:19%
sets to catch up in reps. Whereas with
dynamic double progression, you're

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:58.040 align:start position:19%
maintaining the same effort level across
all of your sets. And so you can see

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:01.840 align:start position:19%
exactly how your strength is progressing
on every set, and you're giving yourself

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:05.520 align:start position:19%
an opportunity across every set to
progress. Now, technically you could use

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:10.120 align:start position:19%
whatever rep range you want for this,
but I generally like to use rep ranges

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:14.160 align:start position:19%
that have a gap of two reps. So, maybe
five to seven reps, uh six to eight,

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.440 align:start position:19%
eight to 10, 10 to 12. Because usually I
find that when you do increase the

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:21.840 align:start position:19%
weight, whether it's five to 10 lb on a
compound lift or two and a half to five

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:25.320 align:start position:19%
lb uh on an isolation, usually you'll
lose about two reps with each increase.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:29.240 align:start position:19%
Uh not always, that's just a general
guideline. Um you can uh use larger

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:34.400 align:start position:19%
gaps, that's totally fine. But if you're
doing uh say six to 10 reps per set,

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:38.200 align:start position:19%
then when you do get to 10 reps, you can
apply a larger weight increase. And also

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:41.840 align:start position:19%
keep in mind that it's not the end of
the world if you misjudge things and you

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:46.160 align:start position:19%
end up outside of your targeted rep
range slightly. So, if your rep range is

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:50.080 align:start position:19%
six to eight, and on the first set you
lift uh 200 lb for seven reps, and you

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:54.080 align:start position:19%
feel like you can still get six on that
next set with 200 lb, and so you stick

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.880 align:start position:19%
with that weight, uh but you only get
five reps, that's not a big deal. It

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:01.360 align:start position:19%
doesn't have to be perfect. Or if on one
workout you do um a certain weight for

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.120 align:start position:19%
seven reps, so the following week you
come back in with that same weight, and

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:07.360 align:start position:19%
you're targeting the upper end of the
rep range, so you're aiming uh for eight

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:10.640 align:start position:19%
reps. If you just happen to be
particularly strong on that day, and

00:09:08.839 --> 00:09:14.120 align:start position:19%
you're able to get nine, then go for
nine. Okay, don't just stop at eight

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:17.640 align:start position:19%
because your rep range on paper is six
to eight reps. The goal is to train one

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:20.920 align:start position:19%
to two reps short of failure on the
majority of your sets, regardless of

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.720 align:start position:19%
what you think you should be getting.
So, this isn't going to be perfect.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:27.400 align:start position:19%
Okay, it's just a general model. Um you
have a given rep range. Once you hit the

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:30.960 align:start position:19%
upper end of that rep range on the first
set, then you increase the weight the

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:34.600 align:start position:19%
next workout on that first set, and then
you adjust the weight if necessary on

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:38.560 align:start position:19%
the remaining sets to stay within your
targeted rep range. And the whole thing

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:42.040 align:start position:19%
just gradually moves forward over time.
Um it's not the only way of doing this,

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:45.080 align:start position:19%
and as you get more advanced, um there
are other ways to apply progressive

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:48.600 align:start position:19%
overload besides just adding more weight
and more reps, but this is a highly

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.040 align:start position:19%
effective way to pack on overall size
and strength, and it's a method that you

